+class CSGRPOAlgoConfig(BaseAlgoConfig):
+    type: Literal["cs_grpo"] = "cs_grpo"
+    """CS-GRPO: sample a matrix of M problems x N rollouts, score POPULATIONS of one rollout per
+    problem with the benchmark's own AUROC and calibration slope, and credit each rollout with the mean
+    score of the populations it joined. GRPO's baseline is unchanged -- the credit is still relative to
+    the rollout's own problem group -- but the reward is a population statistic, which is what the
+    benchmark is judged on and what no single rollout can carry."""
+
+    action_loss_type: ClassVar[ActionLossType] = "rl"
+
+    echo_alpha: float = 0.0
+    """ECHO's lambda on tool-response bodies, or 0.0 to leave the observation tokens untrained.
+
+    Reuses `EchoAlgorithm._weight_observations` rather than restating it: the selection rule (env-provided
+    nodes AFTER the first model response, narrowed to message bodies by the renderer's per-token
+    `is_content`) is the vetted one, and a second copy of it would drift. The tokens feed the `ce`
+    component and stay outside the rl mask and its denominator, so this does not dilute the population
+    term -- `docs/algorithms.md:191`: "adding echo observation tokens never changes the rl term's
+    effective per-token learning rate".
+
+    Why CohortWorld wants it. A legal move id is `axis.op.code->version:16-hex`, unguessable by
+    construction, and it enters the context ONLY in a tool result: `cw_look`'s listing, or the listing a
+    REFUSED `cw_move` returns. Measured on an earlier run: `cw_move` is accepted 120/155 = 77.4% when such a
+    listing is already in context and 0/122 when it is not, and every accepted move in two runs -- 220 of
+    220 -- copied its id verbatim out of one. The thing the policy has to learn is the SHAPE of what the
+    environment says back, which is exactly what a cross-entropy loss on the environment's own tokens
+    teaches. 0.1 is the vetted default (`EchoAlgoConfig.roles`)."""
+
+
+    num_problems: int = 8
+    """M: how many patients a population holds. One is the problem being scored; the other M-1 come from
+    a rolling context of the most recently scored problems, because `score_group` runs on the
+    orchestrator's main loop and waiting there for siblings deadlocks it. This is the sample size of
+    every AUROC the reward is built from -- below ~8 it is dominated by which patients landed together."""
+
+    credit: Literal["contribution", "population"] = "contribution"
+    """What each rollout is paid for. `contribution` -- its OWN term in the population statistic (its
+    pairwise concordance against the opposite-label patients, and its own effect on the calibration
+    slope). `population` -- the whole statistic of the population it joined, which is what the first
+    version paid and which dilutes as the population grows: measured on an early run's first 18 groups, 66.7%
+    of them received no metric gradient at all, because the population AUROC moves in steps of
+    1/(n_pos*n_neg) and one rollout rarely crosses a rank. Kept as an option so the two can be compared
+    rather than argued about."""
+
+    min_population: int = 4
+    """The smallest population a metric may be computed on. Below this an AUROC is decided by which two
+    or three patients happened to land together; those groups train on the format term alone, said once
+    in the log rather than silently."""
+
+    populations_per_rollout: int = 4
+    """How many populations each rollout of the current problem joins. Each uses a different rotation of
+    the context, so a rollout's score averages over several comparison sets instead of one."""
+
+    success_buffer: int = 0
+    """How many past USABLE cells to keep, so a rare success keeps paying after the step it happened in.
+
+    0 disables it. This is the exploitation half of SPEAR's recipe (arXiv 2509.22601, ICLR 2026): an
+    auxiliary tool-use reward to explore -- here the call/move/read ladder -- plus self-imitation, so the
+    trajectories that did the rare thing are reused instead of discarded. Our ladder was already the
+    first half; the second half was missing, and the measurement says that is what costs everything.
+
+    THE MEASUREMENT (an earlier run, 1,488 rollouts over 2 steps, traces read through
+    `vf.Trace.model_validate`): 99.87% declared a rate, 9.07% called a tool, 3.97% moved, 1.68% read a
+    rate elsewhere -- and `read` did not trend up over 5 steps (1.86, 1.56, 1.76, 0.98, 1.56%). A
+    population of M=16 needs `min_population` usable cells, so the necessary condition is
+    `M * p >= min_population`; M*p measured 0.27, and 25 usable cells fell across 96 populations. 12 of
+    12 groups scored 0 cells. The population metric -- the whole point of the algorithm -- contributed
+    no gradient at all.
+
+    WHAT THIS BUYS AND WHAT IT COSTS. A cell read at step t joins the comparison set of populations
+    scored at steps t+1.. until it ages out, so a column with 1-2 real readings can still be measured.
+    The cost is that those members were produced by an older policy version: the statistic is partly
+    off-policy, exactly as SPEAR's replay buffer is, and the depth here is the staleness knob. Credit
+    still goes ONLY to the current step's cells -- buffer members are comparison set, never gradient.
+
+    HONEST LIMIT. SPEAR replays the successful TRAJECTORIES for off-policy updates. That is not
+    reachable from prime-rl's `Algorithm` surface: `score_episode`/`score_group` may annotate only the
+    episodes they are handed, and the train sink compiles those synchronously when `finalize_group`
+    returns, so an algorithm cannot inject a trace from an earlier step. What is reused here is the
+    success's MEASUREMENT, not its tokens. Trace-level replay needs the dispatcher, and is not this."""
+
+    auroc_weight: float = 1.0
+    """Weight on the population AUROC (discrimination): can this ordering of patients be told apart."""
+
+    slope_weight: float = 1.0
+    """Weight on the calibration slope, rewarded as exp(-|log(slope / slope_target)|) so that a slope of
+    3 costs what a slope of 1/3 costs. Zero disables the term."""
+
+    slope_target: float = 1.0
+
+    credit_turn: Literal["first", "all", "action"] = "first"
+    """Which assistant turns carry the advantage. `first` trains only the turn the cold start teaches --
+    the reasoning and the number it commits to -- and gives every later turn zero, so operating the
+    environment produces no gradient of its own. `all` credits the whole episode.
+
+    `first` is the two-stage design: the demonstrations are one turn, and in form A the turns after the
+    first exist because the environment asked for them. Crediting those teaches the policy to answer the
+    ask, which is a crutch; and since it answers by repeating its own reasoning almost verbatim, it would
+    be trained to say the same thing twice.
+
+    `action` is `first` with one exception, and it exists because `first` made the action untrainable.
+    Measured on an earlier run under `first`: nonzero advantages were confined to assistant turn 1 in 800/800
+    trainable branches, INCLUDING all 37 that contained a tool call -- every token after turn 1 is given
+    literal 0.0 and then stripped from the RL loss and its denominator by `_prune_zero_advantages`. The
+    bootstrap ladder was therefore paid to turn-1 PROSE, where it can only upweight wordings that
+    co-occurred with acting and can never upweight a tool-call token. 93.9% of rollouts emitted no tool
+    call at all, and neither the cold start (single-turn demonstrations) nor the reward could teach it.
+
+    Under `action` the three terms go where their claim is about:
+      * FORMAT -> turn 1. The cohort-think block is what has the shape.
+      * LADDER (call / move / read) -> the first turn that CARRIES a tool call; turn 1 when there is
+        none, so a rollout that did not act still receives the ladder's negative centred advantage.
+        Dropping it instead would remove the penalty for not acting and break the group comparison --
+        an advantage that pays acting but does not charge for not acting has no baseline.
+      * METRIC -> the tokens of the committed number, wherever it is written.
+    `first`'s objection stands and is not overridden: a later turn that only RESTATES gets nothing,
+    because it is neither turn 1 nor the turn that acted."""
+
+    call_weight: float = 0.1
+    """Bootstrap rung 1: the rollout emitted any tool call at all. Measured on a pilot run -- 34 of
+    1,338 rollouts called a tool and 1 obtained a reading, so 33 rollouts that took the first step
+    correctly were paid exactly what a rollout that did nothing was paid."""
+
+    move_weight: float = 0.2
+    """Bootstrap rung 2: the rollout reached a different cohort."""
+
+    read_weight: float = 0.3
+    """Bootstrap: pays a rollout for obtaining a reading at another cohort at all. The population term
+    pays only a rollout that moved and read, and the format term is flat once the cold start has taught
+    the shape -- so before any rollout moves, every advantage in the group is zero and the move can never
+    be discovered. Centred within the group like the other terms, so it stops paying as soon as every
+    rollout reads. Set to 0 once movement is established; an early run's step 1 measured num_turns mean 1.002 over
+    512 rollouts, which is why it is not 0 now."""
+
+    metric_weight: float = 1.0
+    """Scale of the population-metric advantage, which lands ONLY on the tokens of the declared number."""
+
+    delta_weight: float = 0.0
+    """Scale of the DELTA-ACCURACY advantage, which lands on the same tokens as `metric_weight`: the
+    declared number's own.
+
+    Default 0.0, so leaving it unset reproduces every earlier run bit-for-bit. It exists because the
+    population metric scores only the ORDERING of `pred = max(reference - declared, 0)` and contains no
+    term for how close `declared` is to the difference it claims. Measured on the 9B CS-GRPO run at step 100: the cold
+    start declared |Adjustment| with median 0.0512, matching the demonstrations' own |true_delta|
+    median of 0.0512, and 100 RL steps compressed it to 0.0002 -- a factor of 256 -- because under this
+    credit assignment a declaration near zero leaves `pred` equal to the reading and preserves whatever
+    ordering the reading already carries, while an honest large declaration clips `pred` to 0 on the
+    65% of external items whose reading is 0 and lands the cell at the bottom of the concordance.
+    Shrinking was the optimum, not an instability.
+
+    The term is deliberately SEPARATE rather than folded into the metric: delta accuracy and the
+    AUROC/slope the metric optimises can disagree, and keeping them apart is what makes the trade
+    observable and ablatable instead of averaged away."""
+
+    delta_baseline: float | None = None
+    """What the delta-accuracy credit is measured against. ``None`` (the default, and what the runs without
+    this term used) subtracts the GROUP MEAN, making the term relative -- and a smoke run measured that
+    this nearly annihilates it, because the policy declares almost the same number in every rollout of a
+    group (median within-group spread 0.0016, the 256x compression): median |delta adv| 0.0019 against the
+    metric's 0.0829, with corr(spread, |delta adv|) = 0.721. A float subtracts that FIXED accuracy instead,
+    making the term absolute, so a group agreeing on a wrong number is still charged. Use 0.5, not 1.0:
+    ``accuracy - 1.0`` is never positive, so every declared-number token would carry a negative advantage
+    and the policy would be pushed to stop emitting the span at all. Cost of a fixed baseline: no variance
+    reduction from the group mean."""
+
+    pred_source: Literal["read_minus_declared", "commit"] = "read_minus_declared"
+    """Where the METRIC term's prediction comes from. ``read_minus_declared`` (the default, and what every
+    run so far used): ``max(reading - declared, 0)``, so the ``cw_commit`` arguments are never read by the credit --
+    which is why the CS-GRPO runs learned ``cw_commit {}`` (evaluation then scores those rollouts on the observed
+    rate, not on anything they stated). ``commit``: the median of the committed ``rates`` (the number
+    evaluation scores as ``stated_commit``, via ``reward.commit_payload`` + ``reward.parse_values``); a
+    rollout with no commit, an empty one or an unparseable one is predicted at the median of
+    ``reward.FLOOR_REPORT`` (0.5) and takes that contribution. The delta term is unchanged: it still reads
+    the turn-1 ``declared`` against ``reading - truth``."""
+
+    delta_scale: float | None = None
+    """Decay constant of the delta advantage: credit = exp(-|error| / delta_scale). DERIVED when unset: the environment's own
+    mean |base_rate - truth| over the training split, i.e. `cal_l2_scale() / T3_CAL_L2_SCALE_MULT`
+    (0.035877 for the frozen item set). A declaration is worth full credit when its error is far below
+    what the base rate itself achieves, and nothing when it is as bad. Not a literal here: restating it
+    would let this scale drift from the one the reward's own q2/r2 are normalised by."""
+
+    format_weight: float = 0.2
+    """Scale of the format advantage, which lands on every sampled token. The format rule is imported
+    from the demonstration generator's own module, so the cold start and the RL objective cannot drift."""
+
+    rate_source: str = "internal"
+    """Which base-rate table binarises the truth. Passed to the environment's own `reward.base_rate`;
+    the threshold and the readable reference are CALLED from the environment (`base_rate`, `entry_rate`)
+    rather than read off the item, because they are not fields on it -- naming fields instead would
+    find none, score no population, and train on the format term alone while looking healthy."""
+
+    tokenizer_name: str | None = None
+    """Where to load the tokenizer that finds the declared number's tokens. Defaults to the policy's own
+    model name, which is the same vocabulary by construction; set it only when that path is not loadable
+    from the orchestrator process."""
+
+    benchmark_repo: str | None = None
+    """Path to the benchmark repository whose `gen/binary_metrics.py` and `gen/demo_quality.py` DEFINE
+    the AUROC, the slope and the format rule. Imported, never restated: a second copy here would drift
+    from the judge the results are quoted against. Falls back to $CW_REPO."""
+
